nna see how that works out but no  promises anyway I thought I'd answer a  few questions so I just tweeted out like  five minutes ago if you guys have any  questions for me so let's just go ahead  and look at the Twitter and answer some  quick questions sir how do you approach  learning an API well when you're  learning an API or you're like learning  how to use a library or something like  that  usually the reason you've like gotten  that library is to saved  development time right because you don't  want to be recreating everything from  scratch so you using a library to kind  of speed that up for you so whilst I  would say that the best thing you can do  is really read all the documentation if  you're talking about like speed and you  want to quickly just get started with an  API usually any good libraries will  include a lot of samples or examples so  you want to take a look at them and see  how you can quickly copy and paste code  and adapt it into your project for what  it is that you're doing specifically  sometimes you won't be able to sometimes  you may need to change things or look at  the documentation or maybe just tinker  around with the code a bit but I would  say just if you're looking for the  easiest way look at the actual samples  that they provide all the example  projects that they provide larger  libraries should have a bunch for each  kind of like major feature they support  so definitely check that out and see if  if you can just basically copy and paste  some code from the examples into your  project off the library and get  something running as quickly as possible  and then from them then you can kind of  work on iterating it and getting better  and improving it for your actual use  case do you plan on doing tutorial  videos on other programming languages  after you're done with C++ if so what  would be the next one so professionally  I really just use C++ and C sharp most  of all I really really likes his sharp I  still thinks a boss boss i